        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.645,
            0.047,
            0.848,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.416,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": "We can say that its length is between $^ { 4 } \\mathrm { ~ \\frac { ~ 4 ~ } { ~ 1 0 ~ } ~ }$ units and $4 ~ { \\frac { 5 } { 1 0 } }$ units. But we cannot state its exact measurement, since there are no markings. Earlier, we split a unit into 10 one-tenths to measure smaller lengths. We can do something similar and split each one-tenth into 10 parts."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.358,
            0.415,
            0.437
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What is the length of this smaller part? How many such smaller parts make a unit length?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.437,
            0.102,
            0.857,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.446,
            0.416,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "As shown in the figure below, each one-tenth has"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.483,
            0.848,
            0.557
          ],
          "content": "10 smaller parts, and there are 10 one-tenths in a unit; therefore, there will be 100 smaller parts in a unit. Therefore, one part’s length will be $\\frac { 1 } { 1 0 0 }$ of a unit."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.572,
            0.767,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.742,
            0.848,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": "Returning to our question, what is the length of the folded paper? We can see that it ends at $4   { \\frac { 4 } { 1 0 } } { \\frac { 5 } { 1 0 0 } } ,$ read as 4 units and 4 one-tenths and 5 one-hundredths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.838,
            0.803,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "How many one-hundredths make one-tenth? Can we also say that the length is 4 units and 45 one-hundredths?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.83,
            0.905,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": "Math Talk"
        },
